{"task_id": "HumanEval/161", "solution": "def _has_letters(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the string contains any letter characters.\n    Iterates through the string to find if any character is an alphabet.\n    \"\"\"\n    for char in s:\n        if char.isalpha():\n            return True\n    return False\n\ndef _reverse_case(char: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Converts a single character's case (upper to lower, lower to upper).\n    If the character is a letter, its case is swapped.\n    \"\"\"\n    if char.islower():\n        return char.upper()\n    elif char.isupper():\n        return char.lower()\n    return char\n\ndef _reverse_string(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Reverses the order of characters in the entire string.\n    Uses string slicing for efficient reversal.\n    \"\"\"\n    return s[::-1]\n\ndef solve(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"You are given a string s.\n    if s[i] is a letter, reverse its case from lower to upper or vise versa,\n    otherwise keep it as it is.\n    If the string contains no letters, reverse the string.\n    The function should return the resulted string.\n    Examples\n    solve(\"1234\") = \"4321\"\n    solve(\"ab\") = \"AB\"\n    solve(\"#a@C\") = \"#A@c\"\n    \"\"\"\n    if not _has_letters(s):\n        return _reverse_string(s)\n    else:\n        processed_chars = []\n        for char in s:\n            if char.isalpha():\n                processed_chars.append(_reverse_case(char))\n            else:\n                processed_chars.append(char)\n        return \"\".join(processed_chars)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/160", "solution": "```python\ndef do_algebra(operator, operand):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two lists operator, and operand. The first list has basic algebra operations, and\n    the second list is a list of integers. Use the two given lists to build the algebric\n    expression and return the evaluation of this expression.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # --- OperatorHandler (src/operators.py components) ---\n    SUPPORTED_OPERATORS = {\n        '+': lambda x, y: x + y,\n        '-': lambda x, y: x - y,\n        '*': lambda x, y: x * y,\n        '//': lambda x, y: x // y,\n        '**': lambda x, y: x ** y\n    }\n\n    OPERATOR_PRECEDENCE = {\n        '+': 1, '-': 1,\n        '*': 2, '//': 2,\n        '**': 3\n    }\n\n    def is_valid_operator(op_char):\n        \"\"\"Checks if an operator character is supported.\"\"\"\n        return op_char in SUPPORTED_OPERATORS\n\n    def get_precedence(op_char):\n        \"\"\"Returns the precedence level of an operator.\"\"\"\n        return OPERATOR_PRECEDENCE.get(op_char, 0)\n\n    # --- InputValidator (src/validator.py components) ---\n    # Per critical requirements, validation functions are simplified to assume valid inputs.\n    def validate_list_lengths(operators_list, operands_list):\n        \"\"\"Placeholder for list length validation.\"\"\"\n        return True\n\n    def validate_operator_list(operators_list):\n        \"\"\"Placeholder for operator list validation.\"\"\"\n        return True\n\n    def validate_operand_list(operands_list):\n        \"\"\"Placeholder for operand list validation.\"\"\"\n        return True\n\n    def validate_inputs(operators_list, operands_list):\n        \"\"\"Orchestrates input validation, assumed successful per requirements.\"\"\"\n        validate_list_lengths(operators_list, operands_list)\n        validate_operator_list(operators_list)\n        validate_operand_list(operands_list)\n        return True\n\n    # --- ExpressionBuilder (src/expression_builder.py compone